  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 244 PM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nAnother MCS traversing locations to the north pushed another outflow \nboundary across the area early today but most locations again have \nquickly recovered with early afternoon temps in the 90s and \ndewpoints in the lower 70s. Objective analysis shows modest surface \nlow pressure over west-central Kansas which will nudge east over the \nnext several hours. Convergence near the low and diminishing CIN \nshould allow for at least isolated storms to form in central Kansas \nand spread northeast in the southwest flow aloft. Shear remains \nsomewhat weak but MLCAPE and DCAPE would support severe downburst \nwinds into the early evening with a decent low-level jet giving some \nsupport as well. Chances for additional showers and storms remain in \nthe forecast into the weekend along and behind the front and any \noutflow. Severe weather potential is a little more nebulous but \ndecent mid-level flow and moderate instability will bring some \nconcern for stronger storms, mainly in the late afternoon and \nevening with better MLCAPE and DCAPE develop. Small storm chances \nreturn in the middle to late part of next week as southerly low-\nlevel flow returns under zonal flow aloft.\n\nHeat indices are in the triple digits most places at 19Z and today \ncould bring the highest such values of this stretch. Over the next \nfew days, outflow and synoptic boundary locations should slowly push \nsouth through the area for slowly cooling conditions. Confidence in \nneither Advisory-level heat in the north Friday nor in the remainder \nof the area Saturday to extended the Warnings is not high enough for \nany headline changes at this point. It's not impossible for elevated \nheat to linger into Sunday if a drier/slower front works out. Monday \nand Tuesday still look the coolest though EPS 850mb temperature \nanomalies point to some warming again Wednesday and Thursday.",
